    <!-- NO VERDICT. This said "PosterChan is not this phone's messages app" on a phone where it
         HAD been made the messages app: `getDefaultSmsPackage` reads the message store's row, which
         Android does not always keep in step with the role it granted. It states what it can see
         and leaves the conclusion alone. -->
    <!-- NO VERDICT. This said "PosterChan is not this phone's messages app" on a phone where
         it HAD been made the messages app: getDefaultSmsPackage reads the message store's
         row, which Android does not always keep in step with the role it granted. It states
         what it can see and leaves the conclusion alone. -->
    <string name="sms_not_default">Showing this phone\'s messages, read from the phone itself.</string>
    <!-- NAMED, not asserted. "1.0.1336 says PosterChan is not this phone's messages app still!" is
         unanswerable while the app only ever states a verdict: a role never granted, a role granted
         in another profile, and the two platform tables disagreeing all read identically. The
         package Android itself reports is the measurement the verdict is derived from, so printing
         it cannot contradict the verdict and it turns "you are wrong" into something checkable. -->
    <string name="sms_default_is">Android says this phone\'s messages app is %1$s, not PosterChan, so texts are delivered there. Set it in Settings \u2192 Apps \u2192 Default apps \u2192 SMS.</string>
    <string name="sms_role_split">Android has given PosterChan the messages role, but its message store still lists %1$s as the messages app, so texts are delivered there. Setting the default again in Settings \u2192 Apps \u2192 Default apps \u2192 SMS usually moves it.</string>
    <!-- A device with no cellular radio cannot be a messages app, and `getDefaultSmsPackage` returns
         null on one — which the "nobody has set a default" branch below would otherwise report as
         something the person should go and fix. It shows what the phone publishes instead. -->
    <string name="sms_no_sim">This device has no SIM, so it cannot be the messages app. It shows the texts your phone publishes to your relay.</string>
